[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatch 1 — broken stdout pipe: Rust exited 0 where C is killed by SIGPIPE
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Status: found and fixed.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Reproduction (read end of the stdout pipe closed before the child writes):
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```python
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m import os, subprocess
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m for p in ["c_src/build/driver", "translation/target/release/driver"]:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     r, w = os.pipe(); os.close(r)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     pr = subprocess.Popen([p], stdout=w, stderr=subprocess.PIPE); os.close(w)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     pr.communicate()
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     print(p, pr.returncode)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Observed before the fix:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | | stdout | stderr | status |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | C | (empty) | (empty) | killed by signal 13 (`SIGPIPE`) → shell status 141 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Rust | (empty) | (empty) | exit code 0 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** The Rust standard library sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` in its
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m pre-`main` runtime initialization. A C program started from a shell inherits
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `SIG_DFL`, so `printf`'s write to a pipe with no reader raises `SIGPIPE` and the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m process dies. In the Rust build the signal was ignored, `write_all` returned
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `EPIPE`, the error was discarded (matching C's habit of not checking `printf`),
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and `main` returned 0 — the same stdout and stderr as C, but a different exit
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m status. A stdout-only comparison would not have caught this; it is exactly the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m "exits 0 where the C exits non-zero" failure mode.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `translation/src/main.rs` now restores the default disposition as the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m first statement of `main`, before any output is produced:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m const SIGPIPE: i32 = 13;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m const SIG_DFL: usize = 0;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m unsafe extern "C" { fn signal(signum: i32, handler: usize) -> usize; }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m unsafe { signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL); }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m After the fix both programs are killed by signal 13 with empty stdout and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stderr. Covered by `stdout_pipe_with_closed_reader_sigpipe`. That test was
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m confirmed to be non-vacuous: commenting out the `restore_default_sigpipe()`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m call makes it fail with `status differs on broken stdout pipe`, and it passes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m again once restored.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Checks that matched with no change required
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Output bytes are exactly `48 65 6c 6c 6f 20 57 6f 72 6c 64 21 0a`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (`Hello World!\n`): one trailing `\n`, no `\r`, no extra newline from Rust's
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `write_all` (a `println!`-style translation would have been equally correct
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   here, but the byte comparison pins it either way).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - stderr is empty for both in every case.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Exit status 0 for every non-signal case.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - stdin is never consumed by either program, including the 1 MiB and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   10 000-line payloads; neither blocks nor reports an error, and the harness
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   tolerates `EPIPE` when writing to a child that has already exited.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Unwritable stdout (`>&-` equivalent, a read-only fd as fd 1): the write fails,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   neither program checks it, both exit 0 with empty stderr.
